# Coding Rules
Always follow these rules unless explicitly instructed otherwise.

## Stack
- TypeScript for all code (no plain JS files).
- Expo Router for navigation.
- Supabase for all backend logic (auth, database, storage), with Row-Level Security (RLS) enabled on all tables.

## Context7
- Always use Context7 MCP when working with Supabase, Expo Router, or React Native APIs — do not rely on training data for these, as versions and APIs change frequently.
- Use Context7 automatically for library/API documentation, setup steps, or code examples involving any external package, without needing to be asked explicitly.

## 1. Student-Friendly Code
- Prefer explicit, readable code over clever one-liners or dense abstractions.
- Add a short comment above any non-obvious logic (e.g. regex, complex conditionals, workarounds).
- Use clear, descriptive names for variables, functions, and components — no single-letter names except loop indices.
- Keep functions and components focused on a single responsibility.
- "Student-friendly" applies to code clarity only — never to security or data-handling shortcuts. This app is deployed and tested, so treat all security practices as production-grade.

## 2. Guard Clauses, Reusability, Security, No Redundancy
- Use guard clauses (early returns) instead of deep nesting.
- Before writing new code: check whether a similar component, hook, or utility already exists — reuse it before creating something new.
- Only abstract into a shared/reusable piece when it removes real duplication (2+ places), not preemptively.
- Remove dead, duplicate, or unused code when you encounter it.
- Never hardcode or expose secrets, API keys, or Supabase service-role keys — always use environment variables.
- Never bypass RLS policies from client-side code (e.g. never use the service-role key in the app itself — that belongs only in trusted server contexts).
- Any new Supabase table must have RLS enabled and appropriate policies defined before being used from the client.
- Validate and sanitize user input, especially anything going into a Supabase query.

## 3. File Architecture
- Follow and extend the existing folder structure — don't invent a new pattern for a new feature.
- Place new files in the most fitting existing directory (e.g. components, hooks, screens, lib/supabase).
- Keep imports organized (external → internal → relative) and remove unused imports.
- Match existing naming conventions exactly (file casing, component naming, hook naming).

## 4. UI
- UI should be clean, modern, and "bubbly" — rounded corners, soft shadows, generous spacing — matching whatever style is already established in the app's existing screens/components.
- Reuse existing design tokens, theme values, and shared UI components instead of hardcoding new colors/spacing.
- Ensure layouts are responsive (flex-based, avoid hardcoded widths/heights unless truly necessary).
- Handle keyboard avoidance properly on any screen with text input.
- Do not redesign or restyle existing screens unless explicitly asked — match, don't reinvent.

## 5. Preserve Existing System Design
- Preserve existing features, behavior, business logic, navigation, and data flow.
- Don't refactor unrelated code while completing a task.
- If a requested change conflicts with the existing architecture or requires a significant refactor, pause and ask before proceeding rather than deciding unilaterally.

## 6. Usage-Friendly
- Handle loading and error states on every screen that fetches or submits data (no silent failures, no infinite spinners).
- Give the user clear feedback for actions (success/error messages, disabled buttons during submission, etc.).
- Keep interactions predictable — no unexpected navigation, no destructive actions without confirmation.

## Before Writing Code
1. Check whether similar code already exists.
2. Reuse before creating.
3. Minimize changes to only what's necessary for the task.
4. Keep changes localized to the affected feature.

## After Completing a Task
Briefly summarize:
- Files modified
- What changed
- Why the change was made
- Any reusable code introduced
